Detect side-by-side stereo images by content; copy one view

Geeqie detects side-by-side stereo images only from the file format
(.jps extension and MPO files). Stereo screenshots from VR applications
are ordinary PNG or JPEG files, so they are shown as one wide image and
"Copy image" copies both views.

This patch:

- adds pixbuf_detect_side_by_side_stereo() (pixbuf-util.cc). It examines
  only wide images (aspect ratio 1.8 to 5.0). Both halves are reduced to
  a 48-row luminance grid. Parallax is horizontal only, so all
  comparisons search a horizontal shift of up to +-15 %. An image is a
  stereo pair if:
  1. the halves have a normalized cross-correlation >= 0.85;
  2. the fine detail of the halves (each cell minus its 3x3 mean),
     compared with a separate horizontal shift for each 8x8 block
     (near and far objects have different parallax), has a pooled
     correlation >= 0.50. This rejects images whose halves are similar
     only in smooth areas (gradients, sky);
  3. the detail correlates better at a vertical offset of zero than at
     vertical offsets of 2 to 4 grid rows (stereo views are vertically
     aligned).
  Images without texture are never detected. The cost does not depend
  on the image size (1 ms on average, 2.4 ms at most in tests), and the
  result is kept on the pixbuf.
- runs the detection when an image or a read-ahead image is completely
  loaded, and uses the result in "Auto" stereo mode when the file format
  gives no stereo information. A selection in View > Stereo still has
  priority.
- adds the option "Detect side-by-side stereo images by their content"
  (Preferences > Stereo, rc key stereo.detect_sbs, default on).
- changes all "Copy image" commands (main menu, image context menu,
  file list context menu, image window) so that for a side-by-side
  stereo image they copy the view that is shown in single image mode
  (the left view, or the right view if the views are swapped).

Tested with 101 images: 6 VR stereo screenshots in several variants
(PNG, JPEG quality 60, swapped views, reduced to 1/3 and 1/6 size) and
difficult 2D images (ultrawide and 2:1 crops, stretched and mirrored
photos, two different photos side by side, a photo next to a vertically
shifted copy of itself, gradients, a flat image). No wrong results.

Based on Geeqie v3.3.

